Lobium - a fingerprint-config profile does not download the speech, translation and TTS components.

THE COST. Every Lobium profile is its own --user-data-dir, and Chromium installs its components
underneath it. A single freshly launched profile, left alone with no user interaction, grows to
824 MB. Measured on 152.0.7977.42, two profiles side by side, the same build:

    component_crx_cache   398 MB     SODALanguagePacks   194 MB
    SODA                  112 MB     TranslateKit         78 MB
    WasmTtsEngine          23 MB     everything else      ~32 MB

Four components account for 407 MB installed, and their CRXs are 385 MB of the 398 MB crx cache -
792 MB of the 824. Because the directory is per profile, it is paid again for every profile the
customer creates.

WHAT IT BUYS. Nothing that a page can see. Probed over CDP on a profile with all four fully
installed, against one that never downloaded them:

    speechSynthesis.getVoices()   0            0
    typeof Translator             undefined    undefined
    typeof LanguageDetector       undefined    undefined
    navigator.plugins / mimeTypes 5 / 2        5 / 2

The two profiles are indistinguishable from web content. The 792 MB is pure per-profile disk.

WHY NOT --disable-component-update. That switch is the blunt version of this patch and was measured
too: it takes the same profile from 824 MB to 3.8 MB. It is rejected because it also stops CRLSet,
First-Party Sets, Privacy Sandbox attestations, Origin Trials, the subresource filter and the PKI
metadata - about 32 MB that DOES shape web-visible behaviour, on a browser whose job is to behave
exactly like Chrome. It is also a command-line switch, so it names itself on chrome://version.

This patch skips only the four heavy ones and leaves every small component registered.

TWO HOOKS, because the four are registered from two places. TranslateKit and WasmTtsEngine come
from RegisterComponentsForUpdate(); SODA is installed from PostProfileInit and gated by
ShouldInstallSodaDuringPostProfileInit(), so suppressing it in registration.cc alone would leave
306 MB of speech models still downloading.

Gated on the presence of --lobium-fp-config rather than on lobium::LobiumFpConfig::Current(), which
is the usual test everywhere else in this series. The two mean the same thing - the config object is
built from that switch - but //chrome/browser does not depend on //components/lobium_fp, and adding
the dependency to reach a boolean would put a build-graph edge in the way of a footprint fix.
base::CommandLine is already available in both files.

diff --git a/chrome/browser/chrome_browser_main.cc b/chrome/browser/chrome_browser_main.cc
index 21d499a926280..6944c198576ce 100644
--- a/chrome/browser/chrome_browser_main.cc
+++ b/chrome/browser/chrome_browser_main.cc
@@ -625,6 +625,13 @@ bool ShouldInstallSodaDuringPostProfileInit(
              ash::features::kOnDeviceSpeechRecognition) &&
          ash::IsUserBrowserContext(const_cast<Profile*>(profile));
 #else
+  // Lobium: a fingerprint-config profile installs no SODA. The speech models are 306 MB per
+  // profile (SODA 112 MB + SODALanguagePacks 194 MB) and expose nothing - a profile carrying the
+  // full install still reports speechSynthesis.getVoices() as 0. See
+  // LobiumSuppressesHeavyComponents in chrome/browser/component_updater/registration.cc.
+  if (command_line.HasSwitch("lobium-fp-config")) {
+    return false;
+  }
   return !command_line.HasSwitch(switches::kDisableComponentUpdate);
 #endif
 }
